# Dart / Flutter
.dart_tool/
.packages
build/
pubspec.lock

# Flutter platform folders are COMMITTED -- app/windows/ and, when they are
# generated, app/android/ and the rest. `flutter create` scaffolds them once;
# after that they are hand-edited source carrying the release identity
# (Runner.rc, AndroidManifest, entitlements, icons) and are reviewed like any
# other file. A clone that regenerates them gets `com.example` back (T-0194).
# What is genuinely generated inside one is ignored by the .gitignore
# `flutter create` writes into that folder -- app/windows/.gitignore excludes
# flutter/ephemeral/, measured at 261.6 MiB in 47 files here. Build output is
# a different path and stays ignored by `build/` above.
#
# Still ignored: .metadata is a tool-state file, not identity. Flutter's own
# convention commits it; see doc/reports/T-0194.md before changing that.
app/.metadata

# Editor state is not committed here, with one deliberate exception: the Dart
# formatting keys in .vscode/settings.json, which are project-scoped tool
# behaviour rather than anybody preferences (T-0323). Everything else VS Code
# writes into that folder -- launch configurations, extension lists, workspace
# state -- stays out, so the exception cannot widen by accident.
.vscode/*
!.vscode/settings.json

# Kotlin Gradle session state, written inside the committed android/ folder by
# a release build rather than under build/, so the rule above does not reach it.
app/android/.kotlin/

# Local working files
collection.review.json
*.xcoll

# Real shelf photos. Never committed: they are pictures of a
# private home, and nothing in the pipeline needs them in git.
#
# The glob, not one name: a second photo directory was created for a
# measurement on 2026-08-22 and `git status` showed it untracked rather
# than ignored, which `git add -A` -- what the publication rebuild runs --
# would have committed. A pattern costs nothing and does not have to be
# remembered at the moment a directory is made.
photos*/

# Secrets. Credentials live in environment variables (CLI) or the OS
# keychain (app) and must never reach a file inside the repo -- see the
# BYOK decision in PROJECT.md.
.env
.env.*
*.env
# ...except the committed reference list of variable NAMES (no values).
!.env.example
secrets.json
# Android release signing
key.properties
*.jks
*.keystore

# Tooling deployed locally on this machine, not part of this repo.
server/
tools/
ui/
agents/
AGENTS.md
CLAUDE.md
.claude/
.githooks/

# The working record of how this was built. Kept on disk, never published --
# it quotes conversations verbatim and is development rather than product.
# What survives publication is the distillate and its evidence:
#   ARCHITECTURE.md      how it is put together
#   doc/decisions/       why it is put together that way
#   doc/measurements.md  what that rests on
# The local tooling reads these paths from disk rather than from git, so
# untracking them costs it nothing.
doc/brief/
doc/reports/
doc/archive/
doc/backlog.md

# The local working rules and the internal state document go with them, and
# for the same reason rather than a different one: much of each is about the
# unpublished record above. A document explaining how to keep a record nobody
# can see is worse than its absence.
# Their durable content is already published -- the decisions in doc/decisions/,
# the numbers in doc/measurements.md, the shape in ARCHITECTURE.md, and what a
# human contributor needs in CONTRIBUTING.md.
doc/conventions.md
PROJECT.md

# The control set is the same: it defines two named sets of private shelf photographs and the recipe for regenerating them. The photographs are
# not published (they are pictures of someone's home), so a document about
# reproducing runs over them is development rather than product. The figures it
# yields are quoted where they are used, in doc/measurements.md.
doc/control-set.md

# And the one decision record whose whole subject is the working record above.
# It says the agent rules are committed and each kind of writing has one
# destination -- true of the development repository, false of the published one
# the moment those files stop being published. A record that describes files a
# reader cannot see is the defect it was written to prevent.
doc/decisions/0013-the-rules-the-agents-follow-are-committed.md
